28/05 14:40 | INFO     | Here’s what went wrong and what to change:

1) Root causes of the failed runs
- Insufficient clearance from the door plane during approach: The insertion/grasp phases were performed too close to the door panel. This caused the door face to occlude/collide, preventing the jaws from enveloping the lever.
- Approach vector not staged from the lever’s free end: Approaching vertically “on top of” the lever encourages interference with the door plane and makes it hard to seat the lever between the jaws. A side approach from the lever’s free end at grasp height is more reliable.
- Grasp verification was minimal: After closing, there wasn’t a robust seating action (a back-and-forth micro slide) to ensure the bar sits between the fingers before applying downward press.
- Unlatch press likely too small: A 2 cm push may be insufficient to depress a spring-latch lever. Increasing to ~4 cm at or near the lever’s tip improves torque about the spindle.
- Door opening path too idealized: A straight outward pull assumes perfect alignment and no arc. Even with a successful unlatch, biasing the pull with a small lateral component along the door width direction better approximates a swing arc and reduces binding at the strike.

2) Specific changes to the plan
- Maintain a larger outward stand-off from the door plane at the start (≥12 cm) and only insert inward at safe height.
- Approach from the lever’s free end: move beyond the tip along the lever long axis, then return slightly along the long axis so the open jaws envelop the bar at grasp height, then close.
- Keep the gripper rotation set to θ = handle angle_long, as requested, so the fingers themselves are parallel to the lever long axis.
- Add a two-phase outward pull: a short “check” pull after the downward press, followed by a longer pull with a small lateral component along the door-width axis to approximate the swing.
- Increase the downward press to ~4 cm for unlatching.
